---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import CommitTypesReference from '../../components/CommitTypesReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Conventional Commit Types Reference" description="Quick reference for all standard Conventional Commits types and which semver bump each implies.">
  <CommitTypesReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why commit types enable automation</h2>
  <p>Conventional Commits' real value isn't just readability — tools like semantic-release parse commit type prefixes to automatically determine the next version number and generate changelogs, removing manual version-bumping decisions entirely.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Pick the type matching the nature of your change.</li>
    <li>Prefix your commit message with it, following the <code>type(scope): description</code> format.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Commit Type Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using "fix" for changes that aren't actually bug fixes</strong>, triggering an unnecessary patch version bump and inaccurate changelog entries.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting the "!" or BREAKING CHANGE footer</strong> for breaking changes, causing automated tooling to under-bump the version (a breaking change should trigger a major bump, not patch/minor).</li>
    <li><strong>Inconsistent type usage across a team</strong> without an agreed reference, undermining the whole point of standardization.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Reference Commit Types</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Setting up automated semantic versioning and changelog generation</li>
    <li>Establishing a team-wide commit message convention</li>
    <li>Understanding what type to use before committing a specific kind of change</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Conventional Commit Types FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What if my change doesn't fit any type well?</strong> "chore" is the common catch-all for changes that don't affect the shipped code's behavior (tooling, dependency bumps, config).</p>
</ToolLayout>
